**Read:** [Common Waste Gas Management and Treatment Systems in the Chemical Sector, BREF WGC](../sources/Chemical-Sector-Waste-Gas-BREF.lingua#r_1TCVADXXV40HYCGK8NTERRSSYA), the complete general inventory, prevention, collection, treatment and monitoring chapters used; [Chemical-industry process descriptions and emission factors — EPA AP-42](../sources/Chemical-Industry-Emission-Factors-EPA.lingua#r_16XA95GZSY1VD7GZNTSXWVNBC3), the whole applicable process/factor and original background sections; [Common Waste Water and Waste Gas Treatment/Management Systems in the Chemical Sector, BREF CWW](../sources/Chemical-Sector-Water-and-Waste-BREF.lingua#r_3DD06XBCANC091G38HBZ4BX7Y9), the full connected air/water and residual-inventory methods.

Gas, vapor, aerosol, mist and particulate releases need distinct physical/chemical descriptions. A species can form in reaction, evaporation, entrainment, degradation, cleaning or an unintended leak. A total release estimate without its activity, material state, control condition and time basis may not describe the process being compared.

Prevention, recovery, capture, absorption, adsorption, reaction and other treatment steps have finite performance and create associated streams. A captured constituent needs a destination; a chemically transformed one needs a product and energy account. Diffuse losses and changed operating conditions can be consequential even when a normal point-source average is favorable.

Compare factor-based estimates, direct measurements and model predictions with appropriate uncertainty and support. An AP-42 factor is a dated average under stated conditions, not proof of a particular plant's current performance. Sampling, calibration, flow/composition and unobserved periods influence actual inference.

**Work:** reconstruct a complete chemical-route air inventory and applicable treatment system from primary methods. Independently balance a consequential constituent and compare measured and factor/model estimates. Evaluate a documented prevention/recovery change, explaining which observations distinguish actual chemical loss reduction from changed sampling, dilution, inventory or transfer to another phase.

